WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.100
In this 5-part series,

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:06.120
I look at the history, benefits,
and safety of water fluoridation,

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.540
and why I changed
my mind about it.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:15.780
"Why Is There Fluoride in Water?
Is It Effective?"

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:17.940
A meta-analysis
of all the studies

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:19.320
on the dental health
implications

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.940
of vegetarian diets

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.160
showed significantly
fewer decayed teeth,

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.180
missing teeth,

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.400
and the number of teeth
with fillings.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:28.980
One of the studies
that bucked the trend

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:31.020
and showed that
vegetarians had more cavities

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:33.480
blamed the excess decay
on the fact that vegetarians

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.520
were significantly
less likely to choose

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.860
fluoride-containing toothpaste,

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.840
supported by the fact
that those who did use

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.880
toothpaste with fluoride
had significantly fewer

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.320
decayed teeth
than those who didn't.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:47.280
Fluoride is thought to protect
teeth by improving the intrinsic

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.100
stability of the mineral
structure of dental enamel.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:53.640
A recent meta-analysis of nearly
100 randomized controlled trials

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.100
of fluoride toothpaste involving
more than 10,000 people

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.020
found "high-certainty" evidence

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.780
that toothpaste containing
the typical amount of fluoride

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.300
reduces tooth decay
significantly more

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:07.320
than non-fluoride toothpaste
in both children and adults.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.500
But what about the addition
of fluoride to the water supply?

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:12.660
A medical consensus

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:14.280
of public health authorities
around the world

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:15.900
has considered
water fluoridation

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:16.800
at appropriate levels

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.360
as a safe and effective means

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.000
to prevent cavities
on a community-wide scale.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.500
In fact, the CDC,

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.840
the US Centers for Disease
Control and Prevention,

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.880
deemed fluoridation
of drinking water

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.640
as one of the top ten
public health achievements

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:30.840
of the 20th century.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:33.420
However, community
water fluoridation

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.360
has long been a flashpoint
of polemic and polarization.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:40.200
Anti-fluoridation activists
underscore that there is

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.420
no human dietary
requirement for fluoride

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.280
and argue that
water fluoridation

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:47.340
isn't effective enough
to justify the costs,

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.320
which may include health risks.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.940
First, some background before
I dive into the controversy.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:56.340
Fluoride is a mineral element
in the Earth's crust

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.560
that naturally occurs
in most water supplies.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:01.320
This is one of the reasons
fluoridation has withstood

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:02.700
Constitutional challenges.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:06.180
Courts have ruled that,
rather than an added medication,

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:08.520
fluoride can be seen as
a nutrient found naturally

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.260
in some areas but not others,

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:11.640
and fluoridation
of water supplies

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.920
is a matter of just
leveling the playing field,

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.600
particularly for
poor communities

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.120
who may have limited access
to dental care.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:20.460
Fluoridation can be defined as

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:22.020
"the upward
or downward adjustment

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:24.000
of the level of fluoride
content in drinking water

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.300
to an optimal level
just enough to prevent cavities

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:30.840
but not enough
to cause fluorosis,"

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:32.820
or cosmetic changes
to the teeth.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.180
It all started in 1901

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.480
with an investigation
into Colorado Brown Stain,

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.580
"grotesque" brown stains

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:43.740
on the teeth
of Colorado Springs residents.

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:46.800
At the same time, teeth
afflicted by the condition were

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.760
"surprisingly and inexplicably
resistant to decay."

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:54.240
After fluoride was identified
as the basis for these changes,

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.480
a nationwide survey of fluoride
levels in drinking water

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.340
in the 1930s
led to the discovery

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:03.000
that fluoride could reach levels
around 1 part per million

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.220
without causing
serious fluorosis.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:07.560
Hoping to get
the best of both worlds,

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:10.320
Grand Rapids, Michigan, in 1945,

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:12.300
became the first city
in the world

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:13.980
to fluoridate its water supply.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:17.460
Within 11 years, cavity rates
among children in Grand Rapids

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.680
were reportedly down
more than 60 percent,

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:23.160
and a fluoridation
movement was born.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.700
Today, about two dozen countries
fluoridate their drinking water.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:29.700
Dozens of others have
naturally fluoridated water

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.820
or instead add fluoride
to milk or table salt.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:37.020
Fluoridation pushback
started almost immediately

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:38.160
in the United States.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.860
The obsession
of Dr. Strangelove's

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:44.460
Colonel Ripper with the purity
of his "precious bodily fluids"

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:48.360
was based on real-life
right-wing Red Scare

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:49.440
conspiracy theories

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:50.940
that fluoridation
was meant to produce

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:55.200
"moronic, atheistic slaves"
who would bow to the communists.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:58.020
Today's social media
is also rife

00:03:58.020 --> 00:03:59.580
with anti-fluoridation messages.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:03.060
Studies have found that 63
percent of Instagram posts

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:05.760
regarding fluoride could be
classified as anti-fluoride,

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.200
64 percent of tweets on Twitter,

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:09.120
99 percent of YouTube videos,

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.000
and up to 100 percent
of fluoride groups and pages

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.100
found on Facebook
were anti-fluoride.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:16.260
Years before COVID,

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:19.560
the antifluoridation crusade
was considered a case study

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.680
of "digital pandemics of
public health misinformation."

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:25.440
In the year before COVID,

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.120
the first worldwide survey

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.400
of the crisis of confidence
in science

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:31.560
of 140,000 people
in 140 countries

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:34.320
found that only
18 percent of humanity

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:36.720
appeared to have a "high" level
of trust in science.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:39.300
To my pleasant surprise though,

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:41.880
despite so many
institutional failures,

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.820
a 2020 follow-up of the survey
found trust in science

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:47.220
actually increased after COVID.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:51.900
Where does the science land
on water fluoridation?

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:54.180
First, let me address efficacy.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:57.600
The CDC attributes the steep
decline in dental decay

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:59.640
in the United States in the
latter half of the 20th century

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:00.900
to water fluoridation,

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:04.740
but similar declines were noted
in non-fluoridated countries,

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:08.100
attributed in large part to the
widespread global distribution

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:09.900
of fluoride toothpaste.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:11.940
The best evidence we have

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:15.000
comes from prospective studies
with concurrent controls,

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:16.740
comparing cavity rates over time

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:19.560
in fluoridated versus
non-fluoridated populations.

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:22.320
There have been more than 100
such studies to date,

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:23.640
and overall,

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:27.600
fluoridation results in
35 percent fewer decayed,

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:28.860
missing, or filled baby teeth

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:30.960
and 26 percent fewer
decayed, missing,

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:31.980
or filled permanent teeth.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:35.640
So, community fluoridation
does indeed help

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:37.080
to cut down on cavities.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:40.500
The question then becomes,
at what cost,

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:43.920
which we'll discuss next.

